PHASE 1: BOARD ASSESSMENT & VOLTAGE IDENTIFICATION
Step 1 — Power state & gross voltage mapping

Meter in DC voltage mode
Black probe → known GND (chassis screw, capacitor negative, shield ground)
Red probe → test points, unpopulated headers, JTAG pads

Common logic voltages:
  3.3V → most modern flash, UART logic
  1.8V → low-power flash, some NAND
  5.0V → older EEPROM, legacy UART
  1.2V / 2.5V → DDR rails, ignore for flash

Step 2 — GND confirmation

Meter in continuity mode
Probe chassis/shield → probe each pin on suspect header
Continuous beep = GND
Mark it. Everything else references from here.

Step 3 — UART pinout identification (4-pin header)

Pins are typically ordered one of two ways:
  VCC | GND | TX | RX
  GND | TX  | RX | VCC

Procedure:
1. Board powered ON
2. All pins probed to GND reference
3. VCC = steady high (3.3V or 5V), no fluctuation
4. GND = 0V
5. TX = fluctuates on boot (0→3.3V rapid transitions) ← KEY
6. RX = steady high when idle (no data being sent to it)

TX will drop to near 0V briefly during boot sequence.
If nothing fluctuates, wait for full boot or trigger activity.

Step 4 — SPI flash identification (for CH341a)

Look for 8-pin SOIC or WSON package
Common markings:
  Winbond: W25Q32 / W25Q64 / W25Q128
  Macronix: MX25L series
  GigaDevice: GD25Q series
  ISSI: IS25LP series

Pin 1 = dot or notch on package corner
Standard SPI pinout (SOIC-8):
  1: CS#   2: MISO  3: WP#   4: GND
  5: MOSI  6: CLK   7: HOLD# 8: VCC

Probe pin 8 → VCC (3.3V)
Probe pin 4 → GND (0V)
Confirm before clipping.

Step 5 — I2C EEPROM identification (24-series)

Look for 8-pin package marked 24Cxx / 24LCxx / 24AAxx
Standard pinout:
  1: A0   2: A1   3: A2   4: GND
  5: SDA  6: SCL  7: WP   8: VCC

A0/A1/A2 = address bits (usually tied to GND or VCC)
SDA/SCL will show activity with meter in AC mode during I2C transactions

PHASE 2: HARDWARE CONNECTION
USB-to-UART (4-pin) — UART console extraction

Adapter TX → Board RX
Adapter RX → Board TX  ← CROSSED, always
Adapter GND → Board GND
DO NOT connect VCC if board is externally powered

Confirm voltage compatibility:
  If adapter is 5V and board is 3.3V logic → use voltage divider on TX line
  3.3V boards fed 5V UART will die

CH341a — SPI flash (25-series)

CH341a in SPI mode (25 series selection)
SOIC clip → chip in-circuit OR chip removed

In-circuit risks:
  MCU may fight the programmer
  Other devices on SPI bus interfere
  Pull CS# of other SPI devices HIGH to isolate

Clip orientation:
  Pin 1 of clip → Pin 1 of chip (dot/notch side)
  Red wire on clip = Pin 1

Connect BEFORE powering programmer.
Board must be FULLY POWERED DOWN when using CH341a on SPI.

CH341a — I2C EEPROM (24-series)

CH341a in I2C mode (24 series selection)
SDA → SDA
SCL → SCL
GND → GND
VCC → VCC (3.3V or 5V per chip spec)

Address pins (A0/A1/A2):
  Match board configuration — probe to GND=0, VCC=1
  Default address: 0x50 (all address pins GND)

