PHASE 1: VOLTAGE MAPPING & PINOUT IDENTIFICATION
GND first — always

Multimeter → continuity mode
Black probe → chassis screw / shield / capacitor negative
Red probe → each pin of suspect header
Continuous beep = GND
Mark pin number. All measurements reference from here.

VCC identification

Meter → DC voltage mode
Red probe → each remaining pin
Black probe → confirmed GND
Steady 3.3V or 5.0V with no fluctuation = VCC
Mark it. Do not connect programmer VCC if board is externally powered.

UART TX identification

Board powered ON and booting
Red probe → each remaining unidentified pin
Black probe → GND
TX will fluctuate rapidly 0V↔3.3V during boot sequence
This is the most distinctive signal on the header
If board has already booted, power cycle and watch during POST

UART RX identification

Remaining pin after VCC/GND/TX are identified = RX
RX idles HIGH (3.3V) when nothing is being transmitted to it
Confirm: TX on adapter → RX on board, RX on adapter → TX on board
CROSSED always — never straight through

SPI Flash (CH341a BIOS25 clip)

8-pin SOIC package — look for Winbond W25Qxx, Macronix MX25Lxx, GigaDevice GD25Qxx
Pin 1 = dot or chamfer on package body

SOIC-8 pinout:
  1: CS#    2: MISO   3: WP#    4: GND
  5: MOSI   6: CLK    7: HOLD#  8: VCC

Probe pin 8 → 3.3V = VCC confirmed
Probe pin 4 → 0V = GND confirmed
Board must be FULLY POWERED DOWN before attaching CH341a clip

I2C EEPROM (CH341a 24I2C)

8-pin package marked 24Cxx / 24LCxx
  1: A0    2: A1    3: A2    4: GND
  5: SDA   6: SCL   7: WP    8: VCC

A0/A1/A2 probe to GND=0, VCC=1 → determines I2C address
Default: all to GND = address 0x50

